<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Lesbos</h1><p class="st-meta">1355 CE – 1462 CE · capital Mytilene</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Genoese lordship of the Gattilusi family on Lesbos, a Byzantine-allied dynasty that ruled northern Aegean islands until the Ottoman conquest in 1462.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Genoese adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-i-gattilusio">Francesco Gattilusio</a> helped the Byzantine emperor John V Palaiologos regain his throne in 1354. In reward John gave him his sister Maria in marriage and the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> as a dowry in 1355, making the Gattilusi hereditary lords of the island, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mytilene">Mytilene</a> as their capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Gattilusi acted as Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and relatives, adopting Greek ways and titles, while trading with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>. Their family extended its power to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aenus">Ainos</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thasos">Thasos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>. They profited from alum, grain and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> trade, and from the 1390s paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Mytilene's castle was rebuilt in their time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453 the Gattilusi remained as Ottoman tributaries. In 1462 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> besieged and took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mytilene">Mytilene</a>; the last lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-gattilusio-lord-of-lesbos">Niccolo Gattilusio</a>, was taken to Constantinople and later executed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1355 CE – 1462 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.67%;width:2.13%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1355 CE – 1462 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mytilene</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francesco I Gattilusio, Dorino I Gattilusio, Niccolo Gattilusio</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Genoese Ligurian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gattilusi Lesbos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=lordship-of-lesbos" aria-label="Lordship of Lesbos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5337 776 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Lesbos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5657 1032L5709 970M5657 1032L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5657 1032h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5657 1032h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mytilene. <a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=lordship-of-lesbos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

